#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124127 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "137818ca-6c48-5ae1-8788-2015f7890ad5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12460" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b550d203e3be327eb2af27a1035b6693953dd2802bb203af06d5773e48d609c3.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = 2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y) = (0,-2),$ so the global maximum point is $(0,-2,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/f80d19126252f6ab7097cf528aec9726c2708f48c815c026f632fccd77cf74d4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124128 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "976e4ec4-93f9-57a4-9f2b-2116e09fed22" :question/math-academy-id "e-12461" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/6c6906429cf946f196b959cce7c20589b6212ae45df9607e15c6385be9aef858.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A, B,$ and $D.$ Hence, $A, B,$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D(x_D,y_D,z_D),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f(x_D,y_D) = f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/cdb83ac891799788c8289f7e44902bf6be008cb13a7bf27f330b48113df021d1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124129 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "94c54743-ba74-51c5-9c59-777125bb76f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12465" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/2ae5b42ae953d1b9d3fd642e9938924a9b2bc439dee1ea52c868f186798b1520.png)\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3,$ what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if $\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ In such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ about $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})\n+\n\\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T\n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ Hence, $\\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124297 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/id #uuid "c6938151-ab77-5288-ba08-156dc942ddec" :question/math-academy-id "e-7030" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/e21f1a654e7ec74a637369e2330d0e0f3ccd0a3469bc15405b9ae581aaa4efba.png) about ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/e2a3ebddc9b6755b15eca1e5497dfc859ea5c2d6bfb5ee0a98df1c6476f87a4f.png) is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/a21655b6405345e52c0f18c50e098409ca06e2b938ee77dc1b839c54f5f60456.png)\n\nwhere \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/a963b613e053f027bc0434bacaba0056c70493b72181eff33f97cd16d39afc37.png) \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/8317d25a4b5a486cf86db32d0868ca8135c4701abf2e2f579b59ce7f4c3ec05b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/5db28cc61ee0aadd0ccc482cacf12c3a851ff803af7927d3bb918bc9c7c72ffc.png)\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/8f3cbdf2ea946537b9792aba2720fb22db787b8b72be93a3c0a5308ff111ee13.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/42f776502d5c552cefc008f7ea701f0f45578326e649b3f4dcd4bc548738d0ef.png) is a saddle point of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/fb7b36c5e6bd7e8c334d8874eb7a781085930a078aadd1cabc79475006fa894c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/7ac5ab8e5444901eb7e978a0916e953bbe6da7ff8fb8a481537ad1f07d5bf48d.png) is a critical point of the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/87f22c24143e0fdad241f6b070b0e0ba1d89b2d5c32669989a7c75df700f5e75.png) if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/2fa6429606f96f4c4f43b91de76acab79152fd9989108836084cea77e0d9db09.png) In such cases, we can classify ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1287cfe4aa57a8ec4c01f84934cb29b3d524f0524a48e39d7f6a30aed97e8ee2.png) as follows:\n\n- if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is positive definite, then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a local minimum\n\n- if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/91ecc7d66921c91ed5baf1b1d2453f9cc4678feed21f325291b38f103f0266b6.png) is negative definite, then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/74bbb71a684ae2b42d8a3779704a0c8b47ef43e5cebd265861418f77cd2b6267.png) is a local maximum\n\n- if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1ada0c06e92bc2e76f33fade0b4f28728f641cc98923f855735c118d2ea0ba25.png) Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/29aa6115eeac65ed8ca2194e8fdce4de48591b144de9ee298e7c151f78bda4ac.png) for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/ffb48b96eb46bc28ce8546552edbc23496ae84720dc2cc4413cc7425b056bba0.png)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/363026acf3cf01ffee6edd4166c849b1cce0cfd1e14af8dc3a15fa2a2f41163d.png) matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/50946bb3afa72276eb132efe89bbe450df8836747fa6d11d04dbb142da7f6fad.png) Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/407d3bd58712c649860e47e39322f4471da8446ed312741900b2697fafe9d4a5.png) (as well as the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/849d81b747384fa1c4d5194327dd049207fdb7bf2db258e117c7214f870555b3.png)) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/a93d47764597bd39da3f5bb625a6d1977c61f6a731e8b041850fef5dc9c7bc52.png) for all ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/8161c7c2b38d84c7033c688b3b41d0decc2861cd218f08dc3f87625eed2cc376.png)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/127d0aace5f471d897e0b6171e68da877234f9b709bf379245032522de9f6aa5.png) we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b18d3603f870bb527a4ee4618ed482165e62659a001cf8f2bbef4503c249b313.png) when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/541f4f6d68d831274235815dceef666c2a59172ab8825d63aa189957530d9ce9.png) is odd, and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/32a94e6ea1436d78a428c8ff22e50a7df311609ab74824c672abd2334c83988a.png) when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/aebdc0a3ecd72c42a452c68c6019eff7ad41de49b8a09517f7fd2ff8df58e32f.png) is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/fcb57eaada70434abbf4a6056c9a8a793f6a0348196813e6a512de4772290f8e.png) but\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/ae6614c135f2e9d1d011de5f58d17fb4e43c2c6b87a7831a57b1287cf1a6e13d.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1851f51854993b0360e75b0fc717263a7a84938b86ae515d74be32a0ac87362d.png)\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/7906e975aaeffa8eae6d319b4be2d6a585c41b95ec59d721eedb7355fda29066.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/3397629141c3034fbd4b5cd5bd8c554676175a4b1e3f5eccd26cc5d66ee1bec8.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) is a saddle point of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/4f118f558380017fb67aa8f0b030dfa06a10216b7381582c619907c32988f211.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cafe3a8a-05a4-508f-8dca-2606a488d835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121400} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ef5011-54dc-5fde-8431-1461516828ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121401} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1dc233-359e-5d16-ae61-f00ab707027c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121402} {:answer/id #uuid "00831521-2e14-5d3f-9047-43ad6d2c38e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121403} {:answer/id #uuid "09f8baf2-9ef7-5987-bebb-73313c3916e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed1dc233-359e-5d16-ae61-f00ab707027c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121402} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8cc3847-5a4c-5ede-b026-8f6a8bc939db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "043d8ef6-a714-5ba6-9a5f-496901cda0dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-162706" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/e1f3255f450e4761e20b64c0782a241a7d73637c9e276fa9c1e07227f26cf4e2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-3.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-3.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ and $(x,y)=(1,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1d257445551687ded71409efdce1b8695a9813a73ecb0fde5b6f4efccc6c15be.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb394be3-36d0-583e-82fb-bd5b65d566db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367177} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc62934-735e-5666-9735-9ac22e67fb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367178} {:answer/id #uuid "be2fd28d-cd93-5c8d-8057-14da0b4a08c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367179} {:answer/id #uuid "3137bd8f-9789-5f2a-829a-a5ad862c09f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367180} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2c5e27-f809-5125-8815-2dc9ce1d25f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a2c5e27-f809-5125-8815-2dc9ce1d25f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367181} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ff43e5-9a36-5921-a5c1-81a8d05c67b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0151a70d-ce12-5172-8abb-638426f2b102" :question/math-academy-id "q-162747" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence $A$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/0a371aa559222bd28c37677bace18eb912bdc978e364bab4cb3d83f7a310de3f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8765f10-c7b0-5ad7-a11c-eef46cd70f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121460} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f6b933-2f0b-5e9d-83dd-b16685eb73ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121461} {:answer/id #uuid "f58312f3-62d8-5c58-96d2-817f5082bebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121462} {:answer/id #uuid "024e0185-9671-5f4e-a5f4-c4fd83ddc179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121463} {:answer/id #uuid "7da88191-9614-5886-b8af-c1d15ac2ad6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da88191-9614-5886-b8af-c1d15ac2ad6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121464} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ae0d45-4a0c-54df-9bf8-4f480562c303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4f668d6-a868-59b1-9e4d-3e52d3a52a67" :question/math-academy-id "q-163723" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-9+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|=20>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=40>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "484fba29-ad87-5941-8a53-43f7352534bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367184} {:answer/id #uuid "4d58a639-4330-588f-a6e1-70732ae91942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367185} {:answer/id #uuid "771004f8-8857-5781-841e-3fe26ffad10c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367186} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae40e71-9967-57fa-97ce-0ea0e77be200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367187} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a56826-c42b-504f-8467-8dc1e2ab4d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "484fba29-ad87-5941-8a53-43f7352534bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367184} :answer-field/id #uuid "740517b9-c4d7-5c42-a662-a02676b9507d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d168a2de-e2b9-56ef-8474-c9a37568dd8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163773" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "715bea73-2a15-5f9c-bd75-3dada3f4a170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367191} {:answer/id #uuid "1805b973-5197-55f4-8b8f-19ea2e135f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367192} {:answer/id #uuid "14a197c9-f7f2-5b26-b427-5576df080cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367193} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0caf17-0145-58c4-be74-1e643be463ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367194} {:answer/id #uuid "efc5ef9b-2b40-501e-95b2-16c1db904823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14a197c9-f7f2-5b26-b427-5576df080cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367193} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2cf4ee5-e508-5fa4-a161-1128ff63e5c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82f753ea-7964-53a3-b90b-5bef2adf907f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163779" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈4+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=5$ and $det(A)=2$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=5,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=5>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183e3535-691b-570e-a2e3-fe769a056a28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367198} {:answer/id #uuid "15f593dc-24e3-587f-a32d-15ad7e54cbb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367199} {:answer/id #uuid "817c24eb-deda-5799-a106-809cee182e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367200} {:answer/id #uuid "3e46d9ba-8e56-58c7-87fb-07269209b82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367201} {:answer/id #uuid "d46b0f80-a0a2-531c-95bc-09ddcf42e64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d46b0f80-a0a2-531c-95bc-09ddcf42e64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367202} :answer-field/id #uuid "c52db7f7-1984-5961-9739-668e55ca9b34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf80131f-4c10-5348-8930-14c545cdb7bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-163780" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=4$\n- $A$ is negative-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54202e6a-ca21-5ed2-9e40-cf632b44eaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367205} {:answer/id #uuid "55b1c31b-3817-5df4-8237-86921347da6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367206} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab45379-3c25-5e55-8ce8-b01155026eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367207} {:answer/id #uuid "38db8491-eba7-5938-adb8-76998d967c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367208} {:answer/id #uuid "d16af734-0eaf-57bd-8bf3-6e05ac780b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54202e6a-ca21-5ed2-9e40-cf632b44eaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367205} :answer-field/id #uuid "444bab0f-2b2d-5995-8132-8c111b8d4dc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a1216b6-1e9a-5f9f-bce6-998f55ac7a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-163782" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -12\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,{\\lambda }_{2}=-16$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-16,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-4<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-16<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =-16<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d417d15-4f51-5d2a-9b0f-291a824d2a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367212} {:answer/id #uuid "493a6275-b46f-5a2e-8f48-a08cc49243f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367213} {:answer/id #uuid "ea55dade-6216-525a-946e-3a6289707806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367214} {:answer/id #uuid "b47b486f-8644-5c82-ba9c-d580ba14bfb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367215} {:answer/id #uuid "3741b59c-a4fa-5bee-93b8-cb654017b94c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d417d15-4f51-5d2a-9b0f-291a824d2a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367212} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d5c1f7-7089-5393-8a46-7a04e9f0ea9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59c2ea9a-a8ee-5204-9030-a96b5132c460" :question/math-academy-id "q-163784" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2-\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2+\\sqrt{2},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-2<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-2-\\sqrt{2}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =-2+\\sqrt{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66a08d21-9e20-5dc7-8693-9747f47f25a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367219} {:answer/id #uuid "07fd156b-7df4-512b-8063-bc909728c83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367220} {:answer/id #uuid "a08b0a06-fe6a-50b6-932b-b6727dad6ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367221} {:answer/id #uuid "be6765cf-4c15-5738-b06d-d353e2139e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367222} {:answer/id #uuid "a00a35ad-2cfa-53d5-9172-fe587106dddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be6765cf-4c15-5738-b06d-d353e2139e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367222} :answer-field/id #uuid "e95208bd-7c1d-5d62-a714-08aa876e0e4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b8937f2-04e6-5039-91a2-37342d44c09a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163785" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,{\\lambda }_{2}=3-\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3+\\sqrt{2},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =8>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3-\\sqrt{2}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =3+\\sqrt{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e1b448c-77c1-53e9-9216-4b32e2b02bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121466} {:answer/id #uuid "f89c515f-ce74-5c10-b07c-a7fec487a792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121467} {:answer/id #uuid "09ed0428-f0f4-5d0b-817b-4de98252672d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121468} {:answer/id #uuid "156f2863-510b-58e7-b0ea-f145b46673da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121469} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca0470d-9920-5323-a3f4-2d35dc402008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f89c515f-ce74-5c10-b07c-a7fec487a792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121467} :answer-field/id #uuid "44aa8dd5-916d-5c5b-96e8-fbbe2448a88d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a10a1d5a-8a2c-54e0-8fb3-61f7b8f41913" :question/math-academy-id "q-163787" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=\\sqrt{6},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-2<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-\\sqrt{6}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =\\sqrt{6}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8e79d92-1e4f-5356-8edc-f419bcbd23a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367226} {:answer/id #uuid "38e0d79b-98eb-5c55-b05f-196979dd7c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367227} {:answer/id #uuid "3db35873-1853-5460-a158-f79ca4c3fa5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367228} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f3a826-8f7c-5d25-8583-ffe374b193f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367229} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2a2949-7385-5c27-83af-84c0af6f2864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3db35873-1853-5460-a158-f79ca4c3fa5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367228} :answer-field/id #uuid "485600a2-9349-5a7b-bd6a-6faca42ba3c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a28cb23f-b8e0-5435-a525-0125a81f2be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163789" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈16+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3)-1(-2)-4(7) \\\\  & =-17<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=5>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-17<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4223607c-12a8-5a81-8a61-c56245afd59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367233} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b05b0f-edb0-5e7c-924d-24fd8862b141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367234} {:answer/id #uuid "ac071e6f-3543-5cb8-a153-e1b55ac5c827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367235} {:answer/id #uuid "03000afe-754d-5dd1-92cb-b6b477f8f29d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367236} {:answer/id #uuid "d2aafe14-e796-500d-af03-5bd70a908eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03000afe-754d-5dd1-92cb-b6b477f8f29d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367236} :answer-field/id #uuid "c351ded6-159d-5c1d-9ebb-de5d1b4c9635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dd7fe5a7-b487-5ac8-904d-7b5106eafa05" :question/math-academy-id "q-163791" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=-19$\n- $A$ is negative-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(11)-1(-3) \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c05d199f-30b5-5416-874f-8a5b2a25f4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,-2)$ and $(2,3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367240} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0ca9d9-f099-5bcb-8fd0-ff66651c7e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,1.2)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367241} {:answer/id #uuid "704fd941-9283-5860-b64d-2500fa8900a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367242} {:answer/id #uuid "0384c97a-6ff5-5e21-92d7-1fcbb309bbda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367243} {:answer/id #uuid "22ef0ad2-4522-5009-8152-65375ad59e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,1.2)$ and $(-2,1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0384c97a-6ff5-5e21-92d7-1fcbb309bbda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367243} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d01273-2d63-5d86-9542-66e1d59f44ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c163c8c-12fc-59a2-9e1d-9f264f8ad03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163834" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/215b322e06280dd01e5858df9a17cd56673b5cdec69521decca205e03771ac21.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=3.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $3.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-2,1),$ so the global maximum point is $(-2,1,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/d5f948483a80f37257b285d48cd1e6bbf1d8a6ba9b66d1fea5edd865c787711a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9a5f9e5-98f9-576b-884f-c72e63a16105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367247} {:answer/id #uuid "e283904a-989c-5ee4-92cd-199912aa4e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367248} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee1343a-8b26-5ab6-a503-070f331d5839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367249} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c33596-96b6-506a-9b8d-74987ecac9b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367250} {:answer/id #uuid "b57d808f-226e-5af3-9f04-fa749b881ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4c33596-96b6-506a-9b8d-74987ecac9b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367250} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad4c03a2-fd01-55d7-98d3-a3f4c416f2b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4f210d2-8a46-5488-9315-a750b379202e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163838" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/784f2cbc652df83239e40dbe830675a4c3ce99c9f51dec913ff2ec1f62612cdc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global maximum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=4.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $4.$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y)=(-3,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/39b694d4ea57e59d97102963009322aeef84d5fa58df07dbc854a73972ad7458.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dbba028-dba5-511a-8c80-1929be23f144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367254} {:answer/id #uuid "7278d7d0-d8ab-5858-824e-891680c8f2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367255} {:answer/id #uuid "3d36b0ed-e2d8-5c34-8f68-1c324abec3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367256} {:answer/id #uuid "ad234b33-e0f4-5f2b-bfa0-119d71157b4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367257} {:answer/id #uuid "dc187e21-3201-5b29-96fe-4a7ab90458e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7278d7d0-d8ab-5858-824e-891680c8f2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367255} :answer-field/id #uuid "08821280-2cd0-50d8-86e5-c1453ad4833c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c94f5db-a52d-5269-9d70-48b35e30ba08" :question/math-academy-id "q-163839" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b99ff5d9d55de34b2cb3e2176e7c4171a5583e1e010626796c3ca2f9d9ed29cb.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global maximum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=10.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $10.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(2,4)$ and $(x,y)=(-4,4).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/bf82159ccb39f6c07fa870abe04911847dcbb240bd1cf5e6be8a1216fa9cf327.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c19d4a-fed8-5b71-a699-da061c5de7f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367261} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf97902-aebc-585a-9066-bc723a6e48d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $C,$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367262} {:answer/id #uuid "12c62ea8-cd39-561f-8802-a108475dd57c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367263} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbbf52d-7e33-57d4-b6be-a6fb91f1d8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367264} {:answer/id #uuid "0eff40e3-4622-5510-8468-614dc30b7fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dbbf52d-7e33-57d4-b6be-a6fb91f1d8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367264} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8886562-bdc8-5364-ac5d-cd4e99a65578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67f80906-33a6-5403-9e06-beaaa9d2cf40" :question/math-academy-id "q-164150" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/fdfae6a5a321d1dbc235d06438847d5a0cc471fa4e31575f36c3967dca239824.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/82291f89bd32f3a3a20d39dfc89de91b2f673bec7c802cebf6d5fa3b4136ace1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "127c03b9-edbe-51ae-895e-9e144d495028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367268} {:answer/id #uuid "0b171218-990a-598a-9739-2354c1645856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367269} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad772c0-24bc-5c96-b12c-3de0e995bd58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367270} {:answer/id #uuid "573dd3df-c887-569e-8006-9667fa646ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367271} {:answer/id #uuid "8246c1a9-9865-59fd-8314-4c70e51b14c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8246c1a9-9865-59fd-8314-4c70e51b14c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367272} :answer-field/id #uuid "074e900e-fe2b-5e9a-a054-fe706293c851" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2740d2d6-bc06-5779-9308-da4a9d560214" :question/math-academy-id "q-164152" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/c460920f6b2516ac8603852e12d15840cca051736555ea43cf15a278ef9e0155.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $C$ and $D.$ Hence $C$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/cc526bb1cf73fb5b5579966e91077ccd096aea475a4cf13196166bd65ac88d7b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a9beef5-215e-57dd-b165-fd9855f91e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B,$ $C,$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367275} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0e669a-ecf7-5dc4-89ac-4ccb5c2caa91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367276} {:answer/id #uuid "994292d6-e7ec-5d83-8fdd-e886cecd445c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367277} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9df7d4-763c-5942-bf7f-7fff5399f72b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367278} {:answer/id #uuid "b09c4f38-c74a-54b2-af6c-cc0dbe6ab24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d9df7d4-763c-5942-bf7f-7fff5399f72b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367278} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c7de956-15d0-556f-a320-00d356ad8d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e88d3b45-dbd3-534c-971c-cb3d3cea4448" :question/math-academy-id "q-164162" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $C.$ Hence, $B$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $B({x}_{B},{y}_{B},{z}_{B}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{B},{y}_{B})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $B$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/6d98be34c848e0fbce571fc572fe9dae244e6ce80b677063fea2e19983e1a2da.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83fb5253-304a-5b7a-ab5e-93a07455ace2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367282} {:answer/id #uuid "354a71fa-f5bc-5ad1-b9e8-a398c041b4c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367283} {:answer/id #uuid "93058205-6480-5aa2-85e4-c30d41bb9faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367284} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc74907-b0fd-5835-a7bf-99d7eaa70d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367285} {:answer/id #uuid "3163c71d-e169-5a64-9209-bea7bf4b1261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "354a71fa-f5bc-5ad1-b9e8-a398c041b4c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367283} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e64a371-2dd8-5f56-86f9-2722bb59fa57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e3accfb-30b6-5bbc-a8c8-456e487631e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-164164" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/9bafb9e41a34d03b70ca2b06bbb8c498f12b51f6e1625ddcd3a95fc4edbbccb6.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence, $B$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b5e614ba1e002bef688b899e9f837ed3199748f02dd41e822340219b0201c492.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec04ce2-0c7d-5552-b6ea-9d1c06fb909c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367289} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c76ce1-dd48-5357-9632-1f6dbec70853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ and $(-2,1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367290} {:answer/id #uuid "242c825a-e6e4-513b-a249-e790b380f43d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$ and $(0,-2,-1.2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367291} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ede4db-e774-5271-8fa4-c52322387059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,-1.2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367292} {:answer/id #uuid "19694ff0-6edb-5402-b06f-3a8588e09ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ and $(0,-2,-1.2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ec04ce2-0c7d-5552-b6ea-9d1c06fb909c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367289} :answer-field/id #uuid "e63e6c34-4aee-5d2e-9936-1deab18ffbc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f2b7292-aabc-55e5-85ad-e3a00ce5e734" :question/math-academy-id "q-164169" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/867fa59781c1732536e984398478996150c3d6b715c9d216bb82a35e0caf27e0.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,1),$ so the global minimum point is $(1,1,-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/9e2c084b2697ada9a957eec3629ac71aea150563a3af37b4f5d0b25e1921559a.png)"}]}
